<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · 1924 · Central &amp; East Asia</p><h1 class="sg-title">National Anthem of the Republic of China</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="zh">三民主義</bdi> / <bdi lang="zh-Latn">Sānmín Zhǔyì</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>The national anthem of the Republic of China (Taiwan), made from a speech that Sun Yat-sen gave in 1924.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Hu Hanmin, Tai Chi-tao, Liao Zhongkai and Shao Yuanchong (delivered by Sun Yat-sen)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Cheng Maoyun</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the Kuomintang and the Republic of China</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Chinese (Classical)</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Whampoa Military Academy, China</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>words 1924; national anthem approved 1937</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words first given in Sun Yat-sen's speech of 16 June 1924 (published before 1930); music by Cheng Maoyun)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The &quot;National Anthem of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>&quot;, also known from its opening as &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-principles-of-the-people">Three Principles of the People</a>&quot;, is the national anthem of the Republic of China, commonly called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>, and the party anthem of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a>. Its text was written jointly by four members of the Kuomintang, Hu Hanmin, Tai Chi-tao, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> Zhongkai and Shao Yuanchong, and was first heard on 16 June 1924 as the opening of a speech by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a> at the opening ceremony of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/whampoa-military-academy">Whampoa Military Academy</a>. Its first line names the Three Principles of the People, Sun's political doctrine, and informally the song is sometimes called &quot;San Min Chu-i&quot;, a name never used on formal or official occasions.</p><p>After the success of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-expedition">Northern Expedition</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> chose the text as its party anthem and held a public competition for music to go with it; Cheng Maoyun won among 139 entrants. On 24 March 1930 many party members proposed that Sun's speech become the words of the national anthem, then the &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> to the Auspicious Cloud&quot;. Because others objected to a party's symbol standing for the whole nation, a National Anthem Editing and Research Committee was set up, and it endorsed the party song. The Central Standing Committee approved the proposal on 3 June 1937, and in the 1940s the song formally became the official national anthem.</p><p>The words are in classical literary Chinese, using old forms of words that modern speech no longer uses, and follow the conventions of the ancient fu style: lines of four characters whose last characters rhyme. In this the anthem differs from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a>'s &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/march-of-the-volunteers">March of the Volunteers</a>&quot;, written a few years later entirely in the modern vernacular. An official English translation by Theodore B. Tu appears in the government's English-language guides to the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><p>The anthem became the national anthem in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> on 25 October 1945, when Taiwan returned to Chinese rule after the surrender of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. After the government of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> retreated to Taiwan and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> was founded, it ceased to be the anthem on the mainland, where the &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/march-of-the-volunteers">March of the Volunteers</a>&quot; replaced it. In Taiwan it is played at the start of flag-raising ceremonies, before the National Flag Anthem accompanies the raising of the flag. For many Taiwanese the anthem carries several, often conflicting, meanings, and some reject it outright as an anachronistic symbol of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a>.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A national anthem in classical Chinese, opening with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-principles-of-the-people">Three Principles of the People</a>, on how the hopes of a new nation and its people may be achieved and kept; solemn and exhortatory.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Chinese</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Chinese</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="zh">三民主義，吾黨所宗；</span><span class="sg-r" lang="zh-Latn">Sānmín zhǔyì, wú dǎng suǒ zōng;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The Three Principles of the People are what our party holds as its foundation;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="zh">以建民國，以進大同。</span><span class="sg-r" lang="zh-Latn">yǐ jiàn Mínguó, yǐ jìn dàtóng.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>with them to found the Republic, with them to advance to the Great Unity.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="zh">咨爾多士，為民前鋒；</span><span class="sg-r" lang="zh-Latn">Zī ěr duō shì, wéi mín qiánfēng;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O you many officers, be the vanguard of the people;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="zh">夙夜匪懈，主義是從。</span><span class="sg-r" lang="zh-Latn">sù yè fěi xiè, zhǔyì shì cóng.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>early and late, never slackening, follow the Principles.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="zh">矢勤矢勇，必信必忠；</span><span class="sg-r" lang="zh-Latn">Shǐ qín shǐ yǒng, bì xìn bì zhōng;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Vow to be diligent, vow to be brave, be sure to be true, be sure to be loyal;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="zh">一心一德，貫徹始終。</span><span class="sg-r" lang="zh-Latn">yī xīn yī dé, guànchè shǐzhōng.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>of one heart and one virtue, carry it through from beginning to end.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Sun Yat-sen, address at the Whampoa Military Academy (1924). Text from <a href="https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E4%B8%AD%E8%8F%AF%E6%B0%91%E5%9C%8B%E5%9C%8B%E6%AD%8C" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (pinyin with tones).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (7)</summary><ul><li>Romanization: Hanyu Pinyin with the citation tones of each syllable (tone sandhi, e.g. of 一 and of two third tones, is not written). 為 in line 3 is read wéi, 'to be, act as'; read wèi it would mean 'for the people, (be) the vanguard'. 'Mínguó' is capitalised as the name of the Republic (民國).</li><li>line 1: 吾黨 'our party' is the Kuomintang, whose song this was before it became the anthem; 所宗 'what (we) take as the ancestral, guiding teaching'.</li><li>line 2: 大同 (dàtóng), the &quot;Great Unity&quot; or &quot;Great Harmony&quot;, the ideal age of the Confucian classics.</li><li>line 3: 咨爾 &quot;O you&quot; is an old formula of address; 多士 &quot;the many officers&quot; or &quot;gentlemen&quot;.</li><li>line 4: 夙夜匪懈 'morning and night without slackening'. 主義是從, 'the Principles (are what we) follow', inverts object and verb with 是 in the classical manner.</li><li>line 5: 矢 here is 'to vow, swear' (its classical sense beside 'arrow'). 信 'true, trustworthy, keeping faith'.</li><li>line 6: 一心一德 'one heart, one virtue'. 貫徹始終 'carry through from start to finish'; 'it' (the cause) is supplied.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
